            "title": "White House weighs next steps after latest China talks - POLITICO",
            "snippets": [
              "# White House weighs next steps after latest China talks\nU.S-China trade talks are pictured. | AP Photo\n\nCommerce Secretary Wilbur Ross (second from left) is in Beijing for talks on China's promise to buy more American goods after Washington ratcheted up tensions with a new threat of tariff hikes on Chinese high-tech exports.",
              "High-level trade talks with China over the weekend produced no immediate breakthroughs, the White House indicated Monday in a bland statement that made no reference to Beijing’s threat to halt the negotiations if the United States proceeds to impose tariffs on $50 billion worth of Chinese goods.\n\n“The meetings focused on reducing the United States’ trade deficit by facilitating the supply of agricultural and energy products to meet China’s growing consumption needs, which will help support growth and employment in the United States,” the White House said",
              "“The United States officials conveyed President Donald J. Trump’s clear goal for achieving a fair trading relationship with China,” the statement said.\n\nThe next line then vaguely hinted at the steps that could come in the next few weeks, as the White House gears up for a high-stakes June 12 summit with North Korea, just a few days before its self-imposed deadline for publishing a final list of Chinese goods that will be hit with a 25 percent tariff",
              "“The delegations will now report back to receive guidance on the path forward,” the statement said.\n\nBeijing was first out of the gate with a statement on Sunday after two days of meetings in Beijing led by Commerce Secretary Wilbur Ross and Chinese Vice Premier Liu He.\n\nChina warned that “all economic and trade outcomes of the talks will not take effect if the U.S. side imposes any trade sanctions, including raising tariffs.”",
              "That was a reference to Trump’s plan to impose a 25 percent tariff on about $50 billion worth of Chinese goods related to a Chinese initiative that aims to make the country dominant in several high-tech sectors like robotics, artificial intelligence and self-driving cars",
              "The Trump administration plans to issue the final list of goods that will be hit with the tariffs by June 15, and impose the duties shortly thereafter. That’s expected to prompt China to retaliate on $50 billion worth of U.S. goods, including leading exports like soybeans and aircraft.\n\nTrump seemed to allude to the possibility of China taking that action in a tweet Monday morning that noted “China already charges a tax of 16% on soybeans. Canada has all sorts of trade barriers on our Agricultural products. Not acceptable!”",
              "Last year, the United States exported more than $12 billion worth of soybeans to China, accounting for more than half of U.S. agricultural sales to that country.\n\nThe purpose of Ross’ trip was described as negotiating long-term contracts with the Chinese to boost sales of U.S. agricultural and energy products. However, the prospects for success seemed dim after Trump revived the threat last week to impose new duties on Chinese goods",
              "Trump could accelerate the tariffs by publishing the final list subject to the duties earlier than scheduled. However, that would further strain relations with China, and perhaps jeopardize the outcome of the meeting between Trump and North Korea leader Kim Jong-un.\n\nBut suspending the tariffs again without gaining any real concessions from Beijing would expose Trump to criticism from Democrats that he is being weak on China.\n\nIn a second tweet, Trump referred to both China and the stalled negotiations with Canada and Mexico over updating the 24-year-old NAFTA pact",
              "“Farmers have not been doing well for 15 years. Mexico, Canada, China and others have treated them unfairly. By the time I finish trade talks, that will change. Big trade barriers against U.S. farmers, and other businesses, will finally be broken. Massive trade deficits no longer!” he said.\n\nIn reality, despite some issues, Canada, China and Mexico are already the three biggest markets for U.S. agricultural exports, with each taking about $20 billion worth of American farm goods",
              "Farmers have been worried Trump could jeopardize those sales by imposing new tariffs on China or withdrawing from NAFTA, as he has often threatened to do.\n\nWhite House press secretary Sarah Huckabee Sanders reiterated that the administration would take steps to shield American farmers from any retaliation as a result of escalating trade tensions, but she offered no specifics of what the measures might look like. In April, Trump directed Agriculture Secretary Sonny Perdue to implement a plan to protect farmers and agriculture interests, but there has not been much discussion about how to do so",
              "“The president has said that he wants to help protect farmers, and we’re looking at a number of different ways to do that,” Sanders said Monday. “We’re going to continue that throughout this process.”\n\nTrump’s trade policies also appear to be leaving him increasingly isolated on the world stage ahead of his meeting later week with leaders of other major developed nations at the annual G-7 summit, which is being held this year in Charlevoix, Quebec",
              "G-7 finance ministers meeting over the weekend failed to agree on a joint statement. Instead, a summary of the discussion written by host nation Canada highlighted concerns over new steel and aluminum “tariffs imposed by the United States on its friends and allies” that “undermine open trade and confidence in the global economy.”\n\nThe six other countries also requested that Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin, who represented the U.S. at the finance meeting, communicate to Trump their “unanimous concern and disappointment,” the statement said",
              "Although Trump argues the steel and aluminum tariffs are necessary to protect national security by enhancing the U.S. defense industrial base, most other countries view the measures as thinly disguised protectionism.\n\nCanadian Prime Minister Justin Trudeau, in a interview on NBC’s “Meet the Press” that aired on Sunday, called the national security justification “insulting and unacceptable” given the close military relations between the two long-time allies.\n\nA senior administration official on Monday characterized comments from Canada and other allies as taking the tariffs far too personally",
              "“I would say simply to the prime minister that these tariffs are not aimed at Canada or China or Russia or any other country,” White House trade adviser Peter Navarro said. “It’s simply a way to defend ourselves, our industries so that we can build the ships and the planes and whatever to defend this nation and by the way, to defend our allies. “\n\nNavarro also argued it was a matter of national self-preservation to impose the duties,"
            ]
